I have all the info I need. Now let me reflect on what the tests need.

Test 1 (loading): 
- render with selectedUserId="p-carla", userPapel="participante", apiClient fake with getCertificados returning a pending promise.
- Expects `screen.getByRole('status')` and text 'Carregando certificados...'.
- Then resolves with [].

Notes: The `getByRole('status')` will match the role="status" element with the loading message.

Test 2 (sucesso):
- getCertificados returns [certificadoEmitido].
- Expects `findByTestId('meus-certificados-container')` and that container has text SA26-7K2M-9QXA, '360', '2', and formatarDataHoraBrasilia(emitidoEm).
- The container must be found via data-testid="meus-certificados-container".

Note: test 1 resolves promise with `[]`, then the loading indicator should be gone. But there's no assertion about that.

Important subtlety: the loading test uses `getByRole('status')`. After the empty resolve, if we render empty-message with role="status", there might be two status elements. But the test only queries before resolution. Still, since the empty-message uses role="status" in existing pattern (CodigoEncontro), the test would pass. But wait — the loading test resolves with empty array and doesn't assert post-state. Fine.

Actually, one catch: in the loading test, after the promise resolves with `[]`, we need the fetch to complete. The `useCertificados` hook should have a `isMounted` guard or not. CodigoEncontro uses a different pattern. useProgramacao and useAtividadeDetalhe use useEffect with mounted guard. Let me follow useProgramacao pattern — it's closest: hook takes selectedUserId, apiClient; uses isLoading, error state.

The guard is in the component: if selectedUserId is absent OR userPapel !== 'participante', render state-panel and do NOT call getCertificados. Since the hook is only called after the guard check (like CodigoEncontro calls the hook after the guard), the API call simply won't happen.

Test 5 (guarda): render with selectedUserId="org-ana", userPapel="organizacao". Expects heading 'Área exclusiva de participantes', text 'Acesso negado ou usuário não autorizado.', and getCertificadosMock NOT called.

The 'Acesso negado ou usuário não autorizado.' is the exact text used by CodigoEncontro/CodigoEncontro guard. Let me match that.

Test 3 (vazio): getCertificados returns []. Expects `findByText(/não possui certificados/i)`.

Test 4 (erro): getCertificados rejects with { erro: 'ERRO_CERTIFICADOS', mensagem: 'Falha ao carregar os certificados' }. Expects findByRole('alert') with text ERRO_CERTIFICADOS and the mensagem.

Now, the hook must:
- receive apiClient injectable with api as default
- call getCertificados()
- represent loading, dados, erro

Following useProgramacao pattern, but note that getCertificados doesn't need selectedUserId. But should the hook accept selectedUserId to decide not to fetch? The task says: "O hook deve: receber apiClient injetável, com api como default; chamar getCertificados(); representar loading, dados e erro seguindo o padrão dos hooks existentes; não conhecer roteamento; não inventar regra de negócio."

The guard is in the component. The component will only invoke the hook after passing the guard, so the hook can just call getCertificados() unconditionally on mount. But to be consistent with existing hooks that accept selectedUserId, should it? The CodigoEncontro hook takes `encontroId` and guards inside. But the guara for CodigoEncontro is in the component (not calling the hook when guard fails). Actually in CodigoEncontro, if the guard fails it returns early and never calls useCodigoEncontro — the hook is called after guard check, at the top level (but after the early return). Wait, hooks must be called unconditionally... Actually in CodigoEncontro.tsx there is an early return BEFORE `useCodigoEncontro`. That's a React hooks violation technically but it's the existing pattern. Hmm, actually let me re-read:

```
export function CodigoEncontro({...}) {
  if (selectedUserId === null || (userPapel !== undefined && userPapel !== 'organizacao')) {
    return ( ... state-panel ... );
  }

  const targetId = encontroId || id;
  const { codigoEncontro, loading, error, ... } = useCodigoEncontro(targetId, apiClient);
```

Yes — the hook is called conditionally after the guard. That's technically against React rules of hooks, but it's the existing project pattern and there's a test for it. I'll follow the same pattern since the component always renders with the same guard condition per mount. Actually since the guard is based on props that don't change between renders typically, it works. But to be safe and consistent, I'll follow exactly what the project does (CodigoEncontro). That's the pattern requested: "renderize o state-panel seguindo o padrão existente" and "NÃO chame getCertificados".

So the structure of MeusCertificados.tsx:

```tsx
import { Link } from 'react-router-dom';
import { useCertificados } from '../hooks/useCertificados';
import { api } from '../api/client';
import { formatarDataHoraBrasilia } from '../utils/date';

interface MeusCertificadosProps {
  selectedUserId: string | null;
  userPapel?: string;
  apiClient?: typeof api;
}

export function MeusCertificados({ selectedUserId, userPapel, apiClient = api }: MeusCertificadosProps) {
  if (!selectedUserId || userPapel !== 'participante') {
    return (
      <section className="state-panel">
        <span className="eyebrow">Acesso restrito</span>
        <h2>Área exclusiva de participantes</h2>
        <p>Acesso negado ou usuário não autorizado.</p>

        <Link to="/" className="button button--secondary">
          Voltar para a programação
        </Link>
      </section>
    );
  }

  const { certificados, loading, error } = useCertificados(apiClient);

  return (
    <section className="meus-certificados-container" data-testid="meus-certificados-container">
      <div className="page-heading">
        <div>
          <span className="eyebrow">Certificados</span>
          <h2>Meus Certificados</h2>
          <p>...</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {loading && (
        <div className="state-message state-message--loading" role="status" aria-live="polite">
          <span className="loading-indicator" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span>Carregando certificados...</span>
        </div>
      )}

      {error && (
        <div className="error-message" role="alert">
          <strong>Não foi possível carregar os certificados.</strong>
          <span>Erro ({error.erro}): {error.mensagem}</span>
        </div>
      )}

      {!loading && !error && certificados.length === 0 && (
        <div className="empty-message" role="status" aria-live="polite">
          <strong>Nenhum certificado emitido</strong>
          <p>Você ainda não possui certificados emitidos para exibir.</p>
        </div>
      )}

      {!loading && !error && certificados.length > 0 && (
        <ul>
          {certificados.map((cert) => (
            <li key={cert.codigo}>
              <strong>Código: {cert.codigo}</strong>
              <span>Atividade: {cert.atividadeId}</span>
              <span>Carga horária: {cert.cargaHorariaMinutos} minutos</span>
              <span>Presenças: {cert.presencas} / {cert.encontros} encontros</span>
              <span>Emitido em: {formatarDataHoraBrasilia(cert.emitidoEm)}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </section>
  );
}
```

Wait, the error test: `expect(alert).toHaveTextContent('ERRO_CERTIFICADOS')` and `toHaveTextContent('Falha ao carregar os certificados')`. The `error.erro` is 'ERRO_CERTIFICADOS' (using `err.erro || 'ERRO_DESCONHECIDO'`). The `error.mensagem` is 'Falha ao carregar os certificados'. So rendering `Erro ({error.erro}): {error.mensagem}` satisfies both.

The success test: `toHaveTextContent('SA26-7K2M-9QXA')`, `'360'`, `'2'`, and formatted date. The code SA26-7K2M-9QXA must appear. '360' must appear as text. '2' must appear — note '2' may match anywhere. `toHaveTextContent('2')` — matches if '2' present anywhere in text content. And formatted date must appear.

Wait, there is a subtle issue: `toHaveTextContent('360')` - the container will have 'Carga horária: 360 minutos' which contains '360'. Good. And '2' appears in 'Presenças: 2/2' or similar. And SA26-7K2M-9QXA appears in the codigo text.

One thing: with the hook, I need to handle empty [] correctly. The hook's initial state — should `certificados` default to `null` or `[]`? If `[]`, then in the loading state, `certificados.length === 0` would be true, but we gate empty rendering on `!loading`. So initial `[]` won't render empty while loading. Also gate error on `error`. Good.

Now, should the hook accept selectedUserId? The component guard ensures the hook only runs for participants with a selected user. Following useCodigoEncontro pattern (hook doesn't take user), useCertificados only takes apiClient. But to be safe and match the pattern "receber apiClient injetável, com api como default; chamar getCertificados(); representar loading, dados e erro", I'll make it just take apiClient.

Actually, wait — should I include selectedUserId in the hook so it doesn't call the API when there's no user? The task explicitly says the guard is in the component and prevents calling getCertificados. Since the component returns early before calling the hook, getCertificados won't be called. Good.

Let me now write the hook using the useProgramacao-like pattern with useEffect and mounted guard:

```ts
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { Certificado } from '../api/types';
import { api } from '../api/client';

export function useCertificados(apiClient = api) {
  const [certificados, setCertificados] = useState<Certificado[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState<boolean>(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState<{ erro: string; mensagem: string } | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let isMounted = true;
    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    apiClient
      .getCertificados()
      .then((res) => {
        if (isMounted) {
          setCertificados(res);
          setLoading(false);
        }
      })
      .catch((err: any) => {
        if (isMounted) {
          setError({
            erro: err.erro || 'ERRO_DESCONHECIDO',
            mensagem: err.mensagem || err.message || 'Erro ao carregar certificados',
          });
          setLoading(false);
        }
      });

    return () => {
      isMounted = false;
    };
  }, [apiClient]);

  return {
    certificados,
    loading,
    error,
  };
}
```

That's clean and follows the existing pattern.

Now let me consider the loading test again: In the loading test, the promise remains pending while they assert `getByRole('status')`. They then resolve `[]` inside `act`, and after `act`, `loading` becomes false and empty renders. But there's a potential issue: if the empty-message also has `role="status"`, then after being pending forever... no. Before resolution, only the loading div exists (certificados still [] with loading true). getByRole('status') finds exactly one. Good.

